NNAAMMEE
     sseennssoorrssdd — hardware sensors monitor

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     sseennssoorrssdd [--dd] [--cc _c_h_e_c_k] [--ff _f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The sseennssoorrssdd utility retrieves sensor monitoring data like fan speed,
     temperature, voltage and RAID logical disk status from the sysctl(2)
     _h_w_._s_e_n_s_o_r_s subtree.  When the state of any monitored sensor changes, an
     alert is triggered.  Every alert logs a message to syslog(3) using the
     ddaaeemmoonn facility.  Optionally, an alert can be configured to execute a
     command.

     By default, sseennssoorrssdd monitors status changes on all sensors that keep
     their state, thus sensors that automatically provide status do not re‐
     quire any additional configuration.  In addition, for every sensor, no
     matter whether it automatically provides its state or not, custom low and
     high limits may be set, so that a local notion of sensor status can be
     computed by sseennssoorrssdd, indicating whether the sensor is within or is ex‐
     ceeding its limits.

     Limit and command values for a particular sensor may be specified in the
     sensorsd.conf(5) configuration file.  This file is reloaded upon receiv‐
     ing SIGHUP.

     The options are as follows:

     --cc _c_h_e_c_k
             Check sensors every _c_h_e_c_k seconds.  The default is 20.  The state
             of a sensor is not yet regarded as changed when a check returns a
             new state for the first time, but only when the two subsequent
             checks both confirm the new state.

     --dd      Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, sseennssoorrssdd will run
             in the foreground.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Read configuration from _f_i_l_e instead of the default configuration
             file _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_n_s_o_r_s_d_._c_o_n_f.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/sensorsd.conf  Configuration file for sseennssoorrssdd.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     sysctl(2), sensorsd.conf(5), syslog.conf(5), sysctl(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The sseennssoorrssdd program first appeared in OpenBSD 3.5.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     Certain sensors may erratically flip status from time to time.  To guard
     against false reports, sseennssoorrssdd requires two confirmations before report‐
     ing a state change.  However, this inevitably introduces an additional
     delay in status reporting and command execution, e.g. one may notice that
     sseennssoorrssdd makes its initial report about the state of monitored sensors
     not immediately, but by default about 60 seconds after it is started.

